# Line endings, settled before they cost anything (#13).
#
# This project is developed on Windows and built on Linux. Without a policy in
# the tree, a checkout on one platform reports every text file as modified on
# the other, and a real formatting defect becomes indistinguishable from that
# noise. The formatting checks are configured to agree with this file rather
# than to fight it.
#
# The default: git decides what is text, stores it with LF, and checks it out
# with LF everywhere. `eol=lf` overrides `core.autocrlf`, so the working tree is
# the same bytes whatever a contributor's git is configured to do, which is what
# makes the policy a property of the tree rather than of each clone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out for the kinds this tree actually holds, so that a reader does not
# have to work out what `text=auto` decided for a given file.
*.rs            text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.lock          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.pyi           text eol=lf
*.h             text eol=lf
*.c             text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.editorconfig   text eol=lf

# Anything whose bytes have to survive a round trip exactly is declared here as
# not text, so that nothing normalises it. Images and archives are the obvious
# cases.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.gz            binary
*.zip           binary

# The conformance vectors of the normal form are the case this policy exists
# for. A canonicalisation fixture whose bytes are rewritten on checkout is a
# fixture that proves the wrong thing, and one that exists to carry a carriage
# return would be silently emptied by the default above.
#
# The normal form decision record, docs/adr/0004-normal-form-first.md, has since
# landed and answers what they need. A vector is line-oriented UTF-8 text
# carrying a case identifier, an input expression and its expected canonical
# output. An index expression contains no carriage return and no trailing
# whitespace that carries meaning, so nothing in a vector is byte-exact in the
# sense this policy is about, and no exemption from it is owed. That paragraph
# of the record is what this declaration implements; conformance/README.md
# repeats the format next to the files a writer will open.
#
# So the directory is declared as text rather than left to the default. The
# default would reach it anyway and the bytes would be identical, and the
# declaration is here so that a reader of this file can tell a directory that
# was considered from one nobody thought about.
conformance/**  text eol=lf

# The bound of the line above, because it is the thing that would go wrong
# quietly. It declares everything under that directory as text, so a binary
# vector format landing there later would be normalised and corrupted rather
# than refused. The record fixes the format as text and gives its reason, that a
# change to a vector has to be readable in a diff by somebody deciding whether
# the normal form moved or a typo landed. Changing the format to something that
# is not text means changing this file in the same change, and this sentence is
# what the person doing it will meet.
